A||N||11||danny boy||'88 front suspension question||||5||
Z||000000||danny boy||02-18-2001||03:49 PM|||| I made another trip to the junkyard today. they now have three '88 coupes. My question is this: Will the front steering knuckle and brakes from an '88 work on an '87? I'd like to do this for the better brakes. Basically I want to unbolt from the control arms and the steering rack and put it on my car.||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||02-18-2001||04:27 PM||||It won't work.  The '88 suspension stuff isn't compatible with the early Fieros ('84-'87) without some serious modifications.  The mount points are completely different.||||reg||1
Z||000002||PJ||02-18-2001||09:31 PM||||What about swappin' the subframes? Will an '88 sub bolt up to an earlier model? Anybody know?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||02-18-2001||09:37 PM||||That's what I was aluding to earlier......the mounting points are completely different.  With a lot of customizing it can be done, but at that point it would be cheaper to just buy an '88 coupe and build it up from there.<P><BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||02-18-2001||09:37 PM||||The engine cradle will on the bottom, but you need adapter plates for the strut towers on top. They used to be available, but I don't think they are now. You may be able to find some if you do lots of research.||||reg||1
Z||000005||danny boy||02-18-2001||10:22 PM|||| I am talking about FRONT spindles. I have an '88 REAR subframe ready to go into my '87 GT. the adapters and coilover kit required is available from Held motorsports.          <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com/</A> <BR> It is not that complicated to put '88 REAR suspension into an earlier car, just elongate the rear bolt holes and move the forward mounts inward slightly.  <BR> I'm not putting his Email because I want everyone to look at his website. If it deals with Fiero suspension, he has it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||02-18-2001||10:51 PM||||A complete '88 front subframe into an early car CAN be done, but it's a LOT of work. Probably 30-40 manhours doing it the first time.<BR>I have done it, and have posted the results here. That post is now in the TD&Q archives and I'm to lazy and tired to look it up (just got done changing the clutch in my '87 GT).<P>How tall are the 88 steering knuckles, from the pivot center of the lower ball joint to the pivot center of the upper? I never measured that dimension.<BR>It might be close to the spindle height on the early car, in which case it could be transplanted with minimal change in suspension geometry. Anoother thing to measure is the distance from the center of the outer tie rod end to the kingpin axis. I don't recall this being radically different, though.<BR>Do '88 ball joints have the same taper as 84-87 ball joints? If they do, then early ball joints could be used in the '88 knuckle. If they don't, then you would have to modify the control arms on oyur '87 to accept '88 ball joints ('88 ball joints are expen$ive!). I don't know how much work this would entail because I have never had examples of early and '88 ball joints next to each other to compare.<P>Good luck. Don't let any of these gain sayers turn you away. Most people who say "it can't be done" have never <I>really</I> tried.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Wannabe||02-18-2001||10:57 PM||||I was considering this exact modification just a few weeks ago. The biggest problem with this swap is the location of the steering rack. The steering arms on the '88 spindles are about 3 higher than on the '87 spindles. It would be possible to accomodate the other differences with custom control arms, but dealing with the rack location is another matter. I'm looking for an '88 now. Somebody want to buy a nice original blue '87  GT?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||02-19-2001||06:45 PM||||The location of the rack shouldn't matter. So what if the arms are 3" higher. That's waht you have tie rod ends for, right? What matters is the distance from the outer tie rod end to the kingpin axis, as this affects steering quickness. On both uprights, the outer tie rod end and both ball joints should be in or close to a plane that is perpendicular to the wheel axis longitudinally, and tilted in by the kingpin angle vertically. This would be affected by ackerman angle, but neither of these suspensions has much of that.<P>I'd say if it's not too hard, try it and see how it drives. If you like it, great! You've found another mod the rest of us can add to our repertoires. If not, then you have, to paraphrase T.A. Edison, found another thing that doesn't work.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wannabe||02-20-2001||12:18 PM||||I don't know Will. At normal ride height, the  steering arms will alreay be angled upward. As the suspension is compressed, the angle will ebcome more critical, causing bump steer and maybe binding of the tie rod ends. I didn't sit down and and do a drawing with accurate measurements, but on first impressions, it didn't look workable. The mounting face on the '88 hubs is about 1.5 -2" closer to the ball joints than the earlier suspension, so it is likely that longer control arms can be fabricated to line everthing up. Unfortnately, as much fun as it might be, I don;t have time to work these things out, so I'm looking for an '88.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Firebug||02-20-2001||12:38 PM||||If you move the steering race vertically 1/4" you can cause bump steer that would be noticable.  Given the light front end of the fiero, the bump steer would be a handful.  Whatever gains you made by putting the '88 parts under the front end would be balanced by the bad bumpsteer characteristics.  You may even end up with worse handling than when you started.  I drive a Mustang Mini-stock for fun during the summer and I've worked with the rack and pinion location and tie rod shims and you can notice real differences in handling.  The best way to describe bump steer is when you go into a corner and the suspension settles down (compresses).  The car tends to pull one way or another and you have to correct a little.  Bumpsteer also creates drag because the toe in/out is changing so the front tires are not always pointed in the same direction.  Bumpsteer is literally a dynamic toe change.<P>Hope I've been of help.<BR>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000011||danny boy||02-20-2001||05:33 PM|||| I'm going to try this anyway. If it doesn't work the parts will be in the Mall soon.||||reg||1
